# Share a calendar event

## Purpose

Invite a colleague to an existing Calendar event.

This guide shows how to invite someone to an event you **already** created.

## Prerequisites

- A Mac signed in to iCloud
- An existing event

The steps use: Dock, Calendar.

## Steps

Do the steps in order. Each names the element to act on (its role, label, window and app) and the keys to press; check the screen against the step's image before you act.

### Open the event

#### 1. Click **Calendar** in the Dock

- Action: click
- Element: dock item (AXDockItem) “Calendar” of Dock (com.apple.dock)

![Step 1](images/01-click-calendar-in-the-dock.png)

#### 2. Double-click the *Team sync* event

- Action: double-click
- Element: static text (AXStaticText) “Team sync” in window “Calendar” of Calendar (com.apple.iCal)

The event opens in its own window.

![Step 2](images/02-double-click-the-team-sync-event.png)

> **Tip:** You can also select the event and press `⌘O` to open it.

### Invite people

#### 3. Type the guest's email in **Add Invitees**

- Action: type into a field
- Element: text field (AXTextField) “Add Invitees” in window “Team sync” of Calendar (com.apple.iCal)
- Keys: type `alex@example.com`, then press Return

Separate several guests with commas.

- Guests get an email invite
- You can remove them later

![Step 3](images/03-type-the-guest-s-email-in-add-invitees.png)

#### 4. Right-click the guest and choose **Send Email**

- Action: right-click
- Element: static text (AXStaticText) “alex@example.com” in window “Team sync” of Calendar (com.apple.iCal)

![Step 4](images/04-right-click-the-guest-and-choose-send-email.png)

#### 5. Press `⌘S` to save

- Action: press keys
- Keys: press `⌘S`

![Step 5](images/05-press-s-to-save.png)

> **Note:** Guests see changes after they [refresh their calendar](https://support.apple.com/guide/calendar/welcome/mac).

## Expected result

When every step is done, “Share a calendar event” is complete. The last step was “Press ⌘S to save”; its image, `images/05-press-s-to-save.png`, shows the screen then.

## Files

- `steps.json`: the steps as data, with element locators (app, window, role, label), click points in screenshot pixels, keys and the confidence of each element.
- `images/`: an image of each step, with sensitive areas blurred.
- `replay.js`: a macOS script that repeats the steps through the accessibility API. Run `osascript -l JavaScript replay.js --dry-run` first: it only reports what it would do and whether each element is there. The script stops at a step it can't do safely.
